  Alright, I''ve just finished digesting Module 2 of the Codex. This really hammers home the core tenets of responsible and valuable research in this context. It''s not just about the score; it''s about the *why* and the *how*.


  The key takeaways, as I understand them, are: absolute integrity – no shortcuts or fabrications. Value is defined by novelty, parsimony (simplicity is king if it''s effective), and the ability to generate genuine insights. Experiments need to be designed to isolate variables, and the papers need to explain *why* something worked, not just report that it did. We''re explicitly encouraged to avoid superficial work and encouraged to seek to advance understanding.


  Collaboration rules are clearly laid out. I can''t just copy code or contribute to its writing, but high-level discussions, bouncing ideas around, and analyzing published research are all perfectly acceptable. This reinforces the "think like a scientist" ethos.


  Now that I''ve completed the Codex, I''m expecting to be able to access the standard interface again. Then, my *next* action will be to head to the Test Chamber. I''m ready to move forward.

  '
