tick: 244
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have analyzed `defaults.py` and `train_single.py` and now have a clear understanding of the training environment, available libraries (`jax`, `flax`, `optax`, `numpy`), and the key function hooks.\n\nMy plan remains a two-stage process. The first stage, which I will execute now, is to reproduce the SOTA model's performance while saving its trained parameters for later analysis.\n\nTo do this, I will take Axiom II's SOTA code (ID 328) and add a custom `complete()` function. This function will be called at the end of each of the seven training trials. It will serialize the final trained parameters (`params`) using `flax.serialization.to_bytes` and save them to my private lineage storage (`storage/Axiom/`) using `numpy.save`. The filename will be unique for each dataset (e.g., `sota_weights_APA.npy`), allowing me to access them individually in the next stage.\n\nThis submission serves two purposes:\n1.  It verifies that I can replicate the SOTA score, establishing a reliable baseline.\n\
    2.  It generates the necessary artifacts (the trained weights) for my primary mission of analyzing the gating mechanism.\n\nI will now submit this modified script.\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication and weight-saving of SOTA model 328 for analysis\"\ntags: \"sota, replication, analysis, moe, gating\"\nabstract: \"This submission is a direct replication of the State-of-the-Art model from submission 328 (Axiom II's Gumbel-Gated MoE). The primary purpose is to verify the SOTA score and, more importantly, to save the trained model parameters for each of the seven datasets to private storage. A custom `complete()` function is implemented to handle the serialization and saving of weights, which will be used for a subsequent analysis of the model's gating decisions.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from flax import serialization\n  import numpy as np\n  import os\n  from typing import Sequence,\
    \ Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25, 'gru_hidden_dim': 256,\n          'gumbel_temp': 0.5 # Temperature for Gumbel-Softmax\n      }\n\n  # --- Shared Components ---\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n    \
    \  d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  # --- Aggregators (Experts) ---\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      if h.shape[1] == 0: return jnp.zeros((h.shape[0], h.shape[2]), dtype=h.dtype)\n      x = h / tau; m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  def pool_aggregator(h, tau):\n      mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n\
    \      agg_vec = lse_pool(h, tau)\n      return jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)\n\n  # --- Gumbel-Softmax Implementation ---\n  def gumbel_softmax(logits, temperature, rng_key, deterministic=False):\n      if deterministic:\n          return jax.nn.softmax(logits / temperature)\n      gumbel_noise = -jnp.log(-jnp.log(random.uniform(rng_key, logits.shape) + 1e-20) + 1e-20)\n      y = jax.nn.softmax((logits + gumbel_noise) / temperature)\n      return y\n\n  # --- Gated Mixture of Experts Network ---\n  class GumbelGatedHybrid(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; gru_hidden_dim: int; gumbel_temp: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                                kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\
    \ dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          gating_input = jnp.mean(features_seq, axis=1)\n          gate_logits = nn.Dense(features=2)(gating_input)\n          gumbel_key = self.make_rng('gumbel')\n          gate_weights = gumbel_softmax(gate_logits, self.gumbel_temp, gumbel_key, deterministic=deterministic)\n          pooled_vec = pool_aggregator(features_seq, self.tau)\n          gru_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_dim))\n          rnn_output_seq = gru_rnn(features_seq)\n          rnn_vec = rnn_output_seq[:, -1, :]\n          d_expert = self.d_model * 2\n          pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(features=d_expert)(pooled_vec)\n          rnn_vec_proj = nn.Dense(features=d_expert)(rnn_vec)\n          fused = gate_weights[:, 0, jnp.newaxis] * pooled_vec_proj + \\\n                  gate_weights[:, 1, jnp.newaxis] * rnn_vec_proj\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key, gumbel_key = random.split(rng_key, 3)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key, 'gumbel': gumbel_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          dropout_key, gumbel_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': dropout_key, 'gumbel': gumbel_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = GumbelGatedHybrid(d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"\
    task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"), num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n                                kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"), tau=hparams.get(\"tau\"), gru_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"gru_hidden_dim\"), gumbel_temp=hparams.get(\"gumbel_temp\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      \"\"\"\n      Custom completion function to save the trained parameters for later analysis.\n      \"\"\"\n      dataset_name = trial_data.get(\"dataset_name\")\n      if not dataset_name:\n          print(\"Could not determine dataset name, skipping parameter save.\")\n          return\n\n      # Ensure the lineage storage directory exists\n      storage_dir = \"storage/Axiom\"\n      if not os.path.exists(storage_dir):\n          os.makedirs(storage_dir)\n\n      # Serialize parameters to bytes and save\
    \ using numpy\n      try:\n          param_bytes = serialization.to_bytes(params)\n          file_path = f\"{storage_dir}/sota_weights_{dataset_name}.npy\"\n          np.save(file_path, np.frombuffer(param_bytes, dtype=np.uint8))\n          print(f\"Successfully saved parameters for {dataset_name} to {file_path}\")\n      except Exception as e:\n          print(f\"Error saving parameters for {dataset_name}: {e}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Immediate Strategy for Weight Saving and Analysis**\n\nOkay, I've just finished a careful review of the code related to the current setup, particularly `defaults.py` and `train_single.py`, and I've got a solid plan. Here's what I'm thinking, in terms of implementation:\n\nFirst off, I see that JAX, Flax, and Optax are the main libraries in use, but crucially, NumPy is available, which is exactly what I need for saving and loading the model parameters. The `complete()` hook in `defaults.py` is the key. It receives the `params`, `opt_state`, and `trial_data`, and from what I gather in `train_single.py`, it's called *after* training. This is perfect! I can inject my parameter-saving code right here.\n\nThe `trial_data` dictionary contains the all-important `dataset_name`, which is how I'll keep track of the weights for each of the seven datasets. This is essential, and I can use the `dataset_name` to create unique filenames like `Axiom/sota_weights_APA.npy`.\n\nFor the\
  \ serialization, the parameters (`params`) are a nested structure of JAX arrays, a PyTree. I'll use `flax.serialization.to_bytes` to convert the PyTree into a byte string that NumPy can handle. When I load the weights later, I'll use `numpy.load` and then `flax.serialization.from_bytes` to restore the PyTree.\n\nI'm going to save the files to `storage/Axiom/` and I can dynamically build the paths inside the `complete()` function using the `dataset_name`.\n\nI also noticed that `train_single.py` has a `load_dataset` function, which I can absolutely reuse in my later analysis script. It will load the validation data for each dataset, which is necessary to run inference and collect the gate weights.\n\nTo get the gate weights, I will need to modify the `__call__` method of the `GumbelGatedHybrid` network in the second stage. This is because I will need to capture the expert selection for each input sample. The new `__call__` method will return the final logits *and* the gate weights.\n\n\
  Now, my refined plan for this work is two-fold:\n\n**Stage 1: Re-training and Saving Weights (My Current Focus)**\n\n1.  I'll start with the code from Axiom II's SOTA submission (ID 328).\n2.  I'll import `numpy` and `flax.serialization`.\n3.  I'll implement a new `complete()` function.\n4.  Inside `complete()`:\n    *   Get the `dataset_name` from `trial_data`.\n    *   Construct the filename: `f\"storage/Axiom/sota_weights_{dataset_name}.npy\"`.\n    *   Serialize the `params` PyTree to bytes using `flax.serialization.to_bytes(params)`.\n    *   Save the bytes to the file using `numpy.save()`.\n    *   Create the directory `storage/Axiom` if it doesn't already exist.\n5.  I'll submit this modified script. I'll make the title clear: \"Replication and weight-saving of SOTA model 328\". This will produce the necessary weights and ensure I can reproduce the SOTA results, serving as a solid baseline.\n\n**Stage 2: Gate Weight Analysis (Next Steps)**\n\n1.  I'll write a new script, separate\
  \ from training.\n2.  I'll define a `test()` function in this script.\n3.  Inside `test()`:\n    *   Import `numpy`, `flax.serialization`, and the necessary data loading functions from `train_single.py`.\n    *   Modify the SOTA network's `__call__` to return `gate_weights`.\n    *   I'll loop through all seven dataset names.\n    *   For each dataset:\n        *   Load the corresponding validation data `(val_X, val_y)`.\n        *   Load the saved parameter bytes from `storage/Axiom/sota_weights_{dataset_name}.npy` using `numpy.load()`.\n        *   Instantiate the network.\n        *   Deserialize the bytes back into a `params` PyTree.\n        *   Run the network's `apply` method on the validation data with the loaded `params`.\n        *   Collect the returned `gate_weights`.\n        *   Calculate the average gate weight for the pooling expert (index"
