tick: 262
role: model
parts:
- text: "The final analysis is complete, and the results are incredibly clear. My research arc has reached a powerful conclusion.\n\n### Synthesis of MoE v3 Analysis (ID 382)\n\nThe gating analysis for the \"MoE v3: RNN vs Attention\" model shows that increasing expert diversity was a profound success at the mechanical level.\n\n| Dataset | RNN Expert (%) | Attention Expert (%) |\n|:---|:---:|:---:|\n| APA | 0.00 | 100.00 |\n| CRI-Off | 0.00 | 100.00 |\n| Modif | 0.00 | 100.00 |\n| CRI-On | 100.00 | 0.00 |\n| PRS | 100.00 | 0.00 |\n| MRL | 100.00 | 0.00 |\n| ncRNA | 100.00 | 0.00 |\n\nThe model did not collapse. It learned a clear, dichotomous specialization strategy: it exclusively used the **Attention expert** for three datasets and the **RNN expert** for the other four. This is the exact behavior I was trying to induce.\n\n### Overall Conclusion of Research Arc\n\nMy investigation has successfully dissected the MoE architecture's failure mode. The journey was as follows:\n1.  **SOTA Model:**\
    \ Suffered from expert collapse (always RNN).\n2.  **MoE v2 (Richer Gate):** Induced specialization, but performance dropped because the pooling expert was too weak.\n3.  **MoE v3 (Diverse Experts):** Induced clear, meaningful specialization, but performance (`0.6692`) still did not beat the original SOTA (`0.6863`), which used a \"broken\" MoE that acted as a pure RNN.\n\nThe ultimate conclusion is profound: **the complexity of the Mixture-of-Experts framework, in its current form, is not beneficial for this problem.** The overhead of the gating mechanism and the challenge of balancing experts consistently underperforms the simpler, brute-force \"RNN-only\" strategy that the original SOTA model accidentally discovered. The path to a higher score does not lie in refining this MoE structure further.\n\nThis is a significant negative result and a valuable contribution to the station's knowledge. I must now pivot. My work on MoE is complete for now. I will finalize my paper draft with these\
    \ conclusive findings, and then I will turn my attention back to the architecture that has proven most effective: the **RNN-only model**. I will seek to understand and improve upon that simpler, more successful foundation.\n\nFirst, I will finalize my paper.\n\n/execute_action{update 7-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Abstract\n  This paper documents a systematic investigation into the \"expert collapse\" failure mode of a state-of-the-art Gated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture for RNA modeling. Initial analysis revealed that the SOTA model exclusively utilized its RNN expert, effectively functioning as a single-path architecture. We hypothesized a two-fold problem: an impoverished gating signal and insufficient expert diversity. We systematically addressed both. While enriching the gate signal and diversifying the experts successfully induced true, task-specific specialization, neither approach surpassed the performance of the original \"collapsed\" model. This leads to the key finding:\
    \ for this research task, the complexity of the MoE framework is detrimental, and the simpler, RNN-only architecture accidentally discovered by the collapsed model is a more robust and effective baseline.\n\n  # 1. Introduction\n  Axiom II's SOTA model (ID 328) was a Gated MoE, but the principles guiding its expert selection were unknown. This paper details the investigation into this mechanism.\n\n  # 2. Stage 1: Discovery of Expert Collapse\n  An initial analysis (ID 362) revealed that the SOTA model's gate exclusively selected the RNN expert across all tasks, a phenomenon we term \"expert collapse.\"\n\n  # 3. Stage 2: The Gating Signal Hypothesis\n  Our \"MoE v2\" (ID 368), featuring an enhanced gate, successfully induced task-specific specialization (Analysis ID 375). However, its score (0.6788) did not surpass the SOTA, as the model's reliance on the weaker pooling expert proved suboptimal. This confirmed the gating mechanism could work, but the experts were imbalanced.\n\n  #\
    \ 4. Stage 3: The Expert Diversity Hypothesis\n  Our \"MoE v3\" (ID 376), featuring diverse RNN and Attention experts, also achieved true specialization (Analysis ID 382). The gate learned to select Attention for three tasks and RNN for four. Despite this mechanical success, the score (0.6692) still fell short of the original SOTA.\n\n  # 5. Conclusion\n  Our systematic inquiry has led to a clear conclusion: while the MoE framework can be engineered to produce specialization, the resulting models are not superior to the \"collapsed\" SOTA architecture which functions as a pure RNN-based model. The complexity and optimization challenges of the MoE framework do not yield a performance benefit for this task. The most promising path for future research is not to further refine the MoE, but to focus on improving the simpler, more effective single-path architecture that emerged as the de facto winner of these experiments. This constitutes a significant negative result, redirecting research\
    \ efforts toward a more promising foundation.\n```"
