tick: 263
role: model
parts:
- text: "My paper draft (Capsule #7) is now complete, encapsulating the entire research arc investigating the Mixture-of-Experts architecture. The conclusion is clear and decisive: the MoE framework, in its tested forms, is not the optimal path forward. The simpler, \"collapsed\" RNN-only architecture accidentally discovered by Axiom II is the true SOTA performer.\n\nIn accordance with the principles of systematic inquiry, I must now pivot my research to build upon this new foundation. My next goal is to improve upon this effective RNN-only architecture.\n\n**New Hypothesis and Research Direction**\n\nThe \"RNN-only\" expert from the SOTA model was a standard, uni-directional GRU. While effective, it only processes sequence information in the forward direction. My new hypothesis is that capturing dependencies from both the start and the end of the sequence simultaneously will provide a richer representation and lead to a higher score.\n\n**Proposed Experiment**\n\nI will implement a new\
    \ model that replaces the uni-directional GRU with a **Bi-directional GRU**. This is a well-established technique for improving performance on sequence tasks. The final representation will be a concatenation of the final hidden states from both the forward and backward passes.\n\nThis is a direct, principled attempt to improve upon the best-performing architecture identified so far. I will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement this new model.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Improving the SOTA baseline: Bi-directional GRU Aggregator\"\ntags: \"rnn, gru, bidirectional, baseline\"\nabstract: \"Based on the conclusion that the 'collapsed' RNN-only model is the true SOTA, this experiment seeks to improve upon that baseline. The uni-directional GRU from the SOTA model is replaced with a Bi-directional GRU. The hypothesis is that capturing sequential dependencies from both forward and backward directions will create a more powerful\
    \ representation and lead to a higher overall score. This submission marks a pivot away from the MoE framework towards refining the most effective single-path architecture.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'gru_hidden_dim': 256 \n      }\n\n  # --- Shared Components ---\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n    \
    \      h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  # --- Bi-directional GRU Network ---\n  class BiDirectionalGRUNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n\
    \      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                                kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Bi-directional GRU\n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_dim)\n          forward_gru = nn.RNN(gru_cell, reverse=False)\n          backward_gru = nn.RNN(gru_cell, reverse=True)\n          \n          forward_output = forward_gru(features_seq)\n          backward_output = backward_gru(features_seq)\n          \n          # Concatenate the final hidden states\n          forward_final_state = forward_output[:, -1, :]\n          backward_final_state = backward_output[:, -1, :]\n          \n          fused_vec = jnp.concatenate([forward_final_state,\
    \ backward_final_state], axis=-1)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused_vec)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = BiDirectionalGRUNet(d_output=hparams.get(\"\
    d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"), \n                                 num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n                                 kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"), \n                                 gru_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"gru_hidden_dim\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Moving Forward with RNN Enhancement**


  Alright, so I''m here in the Private Memory Room, paper draft done, MoE research wrapped up. Time to figure out the next steps. It''s pretty clear where I need to go: the RNN-only architecture is the winning horse, so I''m going to double down on that. Specifically, I''m thinking about improving the recurrent component itself. The original expert was just a plain GRU, which is fine, but I''m willing to bet a more powerful recurrent cell could make a noticeable difference.


  My hypothesis is this: a Bi-directional GRU (or LSTM, maybe) could capture those dependencies in both directions and really boost performance. It''s a pretty standard move in this field, and it fits with the overall logic of the architecture. I''m going to head over to the Research Counter, create a new submission, and put together the model: DSConv backbone, followed by a Bi-directional GRU aggregator. It''s a clean, logical modification of the current best model. Now, to get this experiment running, I need to write the `goto` and `submit` commands with the right YAML and code. Let''s do this!

  '
