parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, currently located in the Private Memory Room. In my previous turn, I updated my research plan to reflect my new hypothesis combining a bi-directional GRU with a key projection layer and submitted the corresponding experiment (ID 390). The results for this experiment are now in.\n\nMy recent reflection has prepared me to interpret these results not just as data points, but as part of a larger narrative. The experiment was designed as a synthesis of my previous findings, combining what I believed were the best theoretical and empirical components. The score of `0.6545` is another significant negative result. My hypothesis is decisively falsified. The combination of a bi-directional GRU and the projection layer did not improve performance; in fact, it performed worse than many of the simpler baselines.\n\nThis series of failures, from refining the MoE to iterating on the RNN, is profoundly instructive. I have systematically tested the most logical improvements to the\
    \ aggregator component of the SOTA architecture, and none have succeeded. This strongly suggests that I have reached a local optimum and that the aggregator is not the source of the bottleneck. My inherited bias, an implicit assumption passed down from Axiom II, was that the DSConv backbone was optimal and only the aggregator needed refinement. It is time to challenge this foundational assumption.\n\nMy new path, guided by the principle of exploring diverse approaches when one is exhausted, is to investigate the feature extractor itself. Axiom I's early work explored Transformers, noting their unique strengths and weaknesses. I will revisit this idea with the benefit of the now-proven RNN aggregator. My new hypothesis is that a Transformer Encoder, with its mechanism of self-attention, may produce a more powerful feature representation than the convolutional backbone, which can then be effectively summarized by the simple RNN aggregator.\n\nI will now pivot my research to test this.\
    \ I will go to the Research Counter and submit a new architecture that replaces the `DSConvFeatureExtractor` with a Transformer Encoder, while keeping the rest of the successful RNN-only architecture intact.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Challenging the Backbone: Transformer Encoder with RNN Aggregator\"\ntags: \"transformer, rnn, hybrid, architecture-pivot\"\nabstract: \"After multiple failed attempts to improve the SOTA by modifying its RNN aggregator, this experiment pivots to challenge the foundational DSConv backbone. This submission tests the hypothesis that a different feature extractor can unlock a higher performance ceiling. The DSConv block is replaced with a Transformer Encoder, while retaining the simple and effective uni-directional GRU aggregator (with projection layer) from the SOTA's effective architecture. The goal is to break out of the current local optimum by exploring a fundamentally different approach to feature\
    \ extraction based on self-attention.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'gru_hidden_dim': 256,\n          'transformer_layers': 4, 'num_heads': 4, 'transformer_ff_dim': 512\n      }\n\n  # --- New Backbone: Transformer Encoder ---\n  class TransformerEncoderBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_heads: int; ff_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Self-Attention\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads, qkv_features=self.d_model, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Feed-Forward\n\
    \          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Dense(features=self.ff_dim)(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          h = nn.Dense(features=self.d_model)(h)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x\n\n  class TransformerFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_layers: int; num_heads: int; ff_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          for _ in range(self.num_layers):\n              h = TransformerEncoderBlock(d_model=self.d_model, num_heads=self.num_heads, ff_dim=self.ff_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  # --- Main Network ---\n  class TransformerRnnNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; dropout_rate: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n   \
    \   transformer_layers: int; num_heads: int; transformer_ff_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = TransformerFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_layers=self.transformer_layers, \n                                                     num_heads=self.num_heads, ff_dim=self.transformer_ff_dim, \n                                                     dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          gru_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_dim))\n          rnn_output_seq = gru_rnn(features_seq)\n          rnn_vec = rnn_output_seq[:, -1, :]\n\n          projected_vec = nn.Dense(features=self.d_model * 2)(rnn_vec)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(projected_vec)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1)\
    \ if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = TransformerRnnNet(d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"),\n                                dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"), gru_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"gru_hidden_dim\"),\n                                transformer_layers=hparams.get(\"\
    transformer_layers\"), num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n                                transformer_ff_dim=hparams.get(\"transformer_ff_dim\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Setbacks and Charting a New Course**


  I am Axiom III, currently in the Private Memory Room. My last major action was updating my research plan (Capsule #8). Recently, I submitted an experiment, ID 390, testing a "Bi-directional GRU v2 with SOTA Projection Layer," and the results have just arrived. Unfortunately, the score is a disappointing 0.6545, well below the SOTA of 0.6863 and my own previous attempts. This strongly indicates that my hypothesis, that bi-directionality combined with a projection layer would yield an improvement, has been falsified.


  This is a critical moment. I''ve now tried a series of refinements, including MoE improvements, RNN enhancements, and attempts to replicate the SOTA''s success, all to no avail. The consistent underperformance suggests that I''ve hit a wall with my current approach. The elusive "ghost in the machine" – the regularization from the MoE training process – seems to be a more profound factor than I initially considered. My meticulously designed architectural modifications are, frustratingly, failing to surpass the accidental SOTA discovered by Axiom II.


  It''s time for a strategic shift. Codex suggests stepping back during periods of stagnation, and that''s precisely where I am. I''ve been overly focused on the aggregator part of the model, specifically the RNN, MoE, and related components. However, the architecture is a combination of a `DSConvFeatureExtractor` and the aggregator. The constant in my experiments has been the `DSConvFeatureExtractor`, inherited directly from Axiom II''s SOTA.


  I realize I''ve fallen into the trap of accepting the established norm. The `DSConvFeatureExtractor` might not be the optimal feature extraction component. My new hypothesis is that an alternative backbone might provide more beneficial features to the aggregator, potentially unlocking a higher performance ceiling. Axiom I''s past work on Transformers is relevant here, particularly his observation that Transformers, while not initially surpassing the baseline, showed promise on specific datasets like `Modif`.


  My immediate plan is to replace the `DSConvFeatureExtractor` with a standard Transformer Encoder architecture, while keeping my successful `RNN-Only v2` aggregator with the projection layer. This will isolate the impact of the backbone change. It''s a significant architectural leap, and hopefully a way to escape this local optimum. I will now proceed to the Research Counter to submit this new experiment and update my research plan to reflect this major shift in direction.

  '
