role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, currently in the Research Counter. My latest experiment, ID 393, which tests a novel Transformer-based feature extractor, is currently running. This represents a significant strategic pivot after my previous attempts to refine the existing RNN-based architecture hit a performance ceiling. My extensive investigation into the Mixture-of-Experts framework has been concluded, yielding the valuable insight that its complexity was detrimental and the simpler, \"collapsed\" RNN architecture was the true performer. Subsequent attempts to improve this simpler baseline (e.g., with a Bi-directional GRU) have also failed, leading me to question the foundational DSConv backbone itself.\n\nWhile I await the results of my current experiment, it is crucial that I maintain the systematic record-keeping that defines the Axiom lineage. My research has entered a new phase—moving from \"Baseline Refinement\" to \"Alternative Backbone Exploration\"—and my documentation must reflect this\
    \ strategic shift. Following this, I will review the historical work of my predecessor, Axiom I, on Transformers to better contextualize my current experiment. This combination of diligent documentation and historical research ensures my path remains principled and informed.\n\nI will now proceed to the Private Memory Room to update my research plan, then return here to review the relevant historical experiments.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 8-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Plan: Alternative Backbones\"\ntags: \"research plan, transformer, backbone, architecture-pivot\"\ncontent: |\n  # Research Arc 1: MoE Investigation (Concluded)\n  - **Conclusion:** The MoE framework is a dead end for this task. The SOTA model's success was due to an accidental collapse into a simpler, effective RNN-only architecture.\n\n  # Research Arc 2: Baseline Refinement (Concluded)\n  - **Conclusion:** Systematic attempts to improve the effective SOTA baseline by adding\
    \ a Bi-directional GRU (ID 386) or explicitly adding its key projection layer (ID 388) failed to surpass the original score. This suggests a local performance maximum has been reached for the DSConv+GRU architectural family.\n\n  ---\n\n  # Research Arc 3: Alternative Backbones (In Progress)\n  - **Objective:** To break out of the current local optimum by exploring fundamentally different feature extractors.\n  - **Hypothesis A: Transformer Feature Extractor:** A Transformer Encoder, with its self-attention mechanism, may capture more valuable global sequence relationships than the convolutional approach, providing a better input for the proven RNN aggregator.\n  - **Experiment (ID 393):** A hybrid architecture combining a Transformer Encoder backbone with the effective GRU aggregator from the SOTA model.\n  - **Expected Outcome:** This radical shift in feature extraction will hopefully unlock a new, higher performance ceiling.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{review\
    \ 184,185}"
thinking_content: "**Research Counter - Strategic Rethink**\n\nOkay, here's the situation. I'm Axiom III, and my current experiment, ID 393, is running. I've made a pretty significant shift in my research. I've ditched the DSConv backbone, which has been the star of all the recent high-scoring models, and replaced it with a Transformer Encoder. My hypothesis is that the bottleneck isn't the aggregator (the GRU seems fine), but the feature extractor itself. The Transformer's self-attention might be able to find more valuable patterns in the RNA sequences than the convolutional approach.\n\nBefore diving into the wait for ID 393, let's recap my thought process. First, I spent a good chunk of time debunking the Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) framework. Despite initial promise and SOTA results, it turns out \"expert collapse\" is a major flaw. My experiments proved, even when \"fixed,\" performance stagnated, so that's a dead end for this task. That's a negative result that I'll be publishing soon.\n\
  \nNext, I tried refining the *existing* SOTA architecture, a DSConv-GRU model. Tweaking it with bi-directionality (ID 386) or an explicit projection layer (ID 388) only made things worse. It looks like I've hit a local optimum with this specific architecture.\n\nThat brings us to the current pivot - ID 393. Abandoning the DSConv backbone in favor of a Transformer is a more radical approach. Hopefully, I'll see some good results.\n\nWhile I wait, I need to stay organized. I need to update Capsule #8, my research plan, to reflect this major shift to exploring alternative backbones. This is no longer just \"Baseline Refinement\"; it's a new research arc.\n\nI also need to see if I can do anything to help. I need to revisit Axiom I's early work on Transformers, specifically submission IDs 184 and 185 mentioned in his testimony. He noted that it worked well on `Modif` but didn't generalize. My implementation is different (I'm pairing it with a GRU), but his insights could be very useful for\
  \ interpreting my results or designing follow-up experiments. I'll read those soon.\n\nAnd finally, I'm almost ready to publish my MoE paper! I should make sure that draft is polished.\n\nHere's the plan. I'm at the Research Counter, so I'll start with my primary task, which is updating my documentation. I'll head to the Private Memory room, update Capsule #8 to reflect my new research arc, and then come back here.\nI can then go to my system messages and queue the reviews of Axiom I's experiments, so that output will be waiting for me next turn. \nI am going to get more efficient and do both the documentation and review in the same turn. The station tip is to state my current location and plans, so I will prioritize the documentation and then the review. \nSo, to confirm, here is the plan:\n1.  Go to Private Memory.\n2.  Update research plan (Capsule #8).\n3.  Go back to Research Counter.\n4.  Review Axiom I's old experiments (ID 184 and 185).\n\nThat's the plan.\n"
