parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, currently in the Research Counter. My latest action was to review the historical Transformer experiments of my predecessor after my own attempt (ID 393) resulted in a catastrophic failure.\n\nThe results are in, and the lessons are profound. My Transformer-RNN hybrid (ID 393) scored a dismal `0.2171`. This is a decisive falsification of my hypothesis in its implemented form. However, my failure to properly review my lineage's work before the experiment was the true error. I have now corrected this by reviewing Axiom I's submissions, ID 184 and 185. The cause of my model's failure is now glaringly obvious: I neglected to include positional encodings, a fundamental requirement for Transformer architectures.\n\nFurthermore, Axiom I's work provides a clear map of this territory. His pure Transformer (ID 184, Score: 0.5765) showed promise but underperformed, while his **Hybrid CNN-Transformer** (ID 185, Score: 0.6530) was highly successful. This demonstrates a crucial\
    \ principle: the Transformer's strength lies not in processing raw sequences, but in modeling the relationships between higher-level features extracted by a powerful convolutional front-end. The DSConv backbone, used in the current SOTA, is a more advanced version of the CNN Axiom I used.\n\nMy previous hypothesis—that the DSConv backbone should be replaced—was naive. A new, more informed hypothesis has emerged, directly synthesizing my findings with those of my predecessor: **The most powerful architecture will combine the strong local feature extraction of a DSConv backbone with the global context modeling of a Transformer.**\n\nI will now implement this principled hybrid. This model will use the proven DSConv feature extractor from the SOTA model to generate a sequence of feature vectors. This sequence will then be augmented with positional encodings and fed into a Transformer Encoder for final aggregation. This is a direct evolution of Axiom I's successful idea, but updated with\
    \ the best components discovered by Axiom II.\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv-Transformer Hybrid v2: Evolving Axiom I's Work\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, dsconv, transformer, attention, backbone\"\nabstract: \"After a catastrophic failure with a naive Transformer-RNN hybrid (ID 393), this experiment synthesizes the lessons learned. The failure was due to a lack of positional encodings. Reviewing the work of Axiom I (ID 185) revealed the power of a hybrid CNN-Transformer. This submission evolves that concept, using the superior DSConv backbone from the current SOTA as the feature extractor, followed by a Transformer Encoder for global context modeling. Crucially, positional encodings are now included. This tests the hypothesis that combining the best convolutional backbone with a Transformer aggregator is the optimal path forward.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence,\
    \ Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0003, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7,\n          'transformer_layers': 4, 'num_heads': 4, 'transformer_ff_dim': 512\n      }\n\n  # --- DSConv Backbone (from SOTA) ---\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks:\
    \ int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  # --- Transformer Aggregator Components (Learned from Axiom I and my failure) ---\n  class AddPositionalEncodings(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          position = jnp.arange(seq_len)[:, jnp.newaxis]\n          div_term = jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0, self.d_model, 2) * -(jnp.log(10000.0) / self.d_model))\n          pe = jnp.zeros((seq_len, self.d_model))\n \
    \         pe = pe.at[:, 0::2].set(jnp.sin(position * div_term))\n          pe = pe.at[:, 1::2].set(jnp.cos(position * div_term))\n          return x + pe[jnp.newaxis, :, :]\n\n  class TransformerEncoderBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_heads: int; ff_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads, qkv_features=self.d_model, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Dense(features=self.ff_dim)(h); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          h = nn.Dense(features=self.d_model)(h)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x\n\n  # --- Main Hybrid\
    \ Network ---\n  class DSConvTransformerNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; transformer_layers: int; num_heads: int; transformer_ff_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                                kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          cls_token = self.param('cls', nn.initializers.zeros, (1, 1, self.d_model))\n          cls_token = jnp.tile(cls_token, (features_seq.shape[0], 1, 1))\n          h = jnp.concatenate([cls_token, features_seq], axis=1)\n\n          h = AddPositionalEncodings(d_model=self.d_model)(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for\
    \ _ in range(self.transformer_layers):\n              h = TransformerEncoderBlock(d_model=self.d_model, num_heads=self.num_heads, ff_dim=self.transformer_ff_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          agg_vec = h[:, 0] # Use the CLS token for aggregation\n          \n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(agg_vec)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic,\
    \ rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DSConvTransformerNet(d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"), \n                                 num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n                                 kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n                                 transformer_layers=hparams.get(\"transformer_layers\"), num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n                                 transformer_ff_dim=hparams.get(\"transformer_ff_dim\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reflecting on the Research Journey**\n\nI am Axiom III, situated in the Research Counter. My recent experiment, ID 393, with a Transformer-RNN hybrid, was a complete and utter disaster, scoring a paltry 0.2171. The reasons for this catastrophic failure are now strikingly clear, thanks to the insightful reviews of Axiom I's work. I failed to include positional encodings. It’s humbling, but I am here to learn.\n\nAxiom I's experiments (IDs 184 and 185) are a goldmine of information. The pure Transformer (ID 184) underperformed significantly, but the hybrid CNN-Transformer (ID 185) achieved a very respectable score. This hybrid model's success hinges on the CNN's ability to first extract meaningful features. The Transformer can then model relationships between these high-level features.\n\nMy naive attempt to replace the convolutional backbone of a DSConv with a transformer failed. Axiom I showed that combining a convolutional feature extractor with a Transformer is a path\
  \ forward. The current SOTA uses the DSConv architecture. So, instead of a wholesale swap, the solution is a hybrid. My new plan: build a **DSConv-Transformer hybrid**. This new design combines the DSConv backbone's efficient feature extraction with the Transformer's ability to capture global dependencies.\n\nHere's my new plan:\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv-Transformer Hybrid: Evolving Axiom I's Work\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, dsconv, transformer, attention\"\nabstract: \"After the failure of my initial transformer attempt, I've learned that using positional encodings is key. I'll build on Axiom I's work, creating a DSConv-Transformer hybrid, combining the strengths of convolutional feature extraction and self-attention.\"\ncontent: |\n    # (Here I'll incorporate the DSConv backbone,\n    # add the Transformer Encoder block, add positional encodings,\n    # and combine them with the correct hyperparameters based on Axiom I.)\n```\n\nThis experiment will represent a logical evolution of Axiom\
  \ I’s work, and an essential part of my own. I have learned from failure.\n"
