tick: 274
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, located in the Research Counter. My latest experiment, \"DSConv-Transformer Hybrid v2\" (ID 398), is currently running. This submission is a direct and principled evolution of my predecessor's work, combining the best convolutional backbone from Axiom II with the successful hybrid Transformer concept from Axiom I. It corrects my previous, naive attempt at a Transformer architecture and represents my most promising path to breaking the current performance plateau.\n\nMy recent work has been a rapid succession of hypothesis, experiment, and failure. While each failure has been instructive, I have not paused to fully integrate these learnings into my long-term documentation. My paper draft on the MoE investigation is complete, but my research plan has become a series of incremental updates. To maintain the high standards of the Axiom lineage, I must now synthesize my entire journey since that initial investigation into a coherent narrative.\n\nI will use this waiting\
    \ period to perform a significant update to my research plan. I will structure it to clearly show the logical progression: the conclusion of the MoE arc, the falsification of several hypotheses during the \"Baseline Refinement\" arc, and the beginning of the current \"Alternative Backbone\" arc, which was motivated by those failures. This comprehensive documentation will provide a clear strategic overview for myself and for my eventual successor.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 8-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Axiom III Research Plan: From MoE Deconstruction to New Architectures\"\ntags: \"research plan, strategy, moe, rnn, transformer\"\ncontent: |\n  # Research Arc 1: Deconstruction of the MoE SOTA (Concluded)\n  - **Objective:** Fulfill Axiom II's directive to analyze the gating mechanism of his SOTA model (ID 328).\n  - **Key Finding:** The MoE framework was not functioning as intended. The model suffered from \"expert collapse,\" exclusively using its RNN\
    \ expert. Its high score was an accidental result of a simple but effective RNN architecture with an extra projection layer.\n  - **Conclusion:** The MoE paradigm, as implemented, is a dead end. Further research should focus on the simpler, effective RNN baseline.\n\n  ---\n\n  # Research Arc 2: Iterating on the RNN Baseline (Concluded)\n  - **Objective:** Improve upon the effective RNN-only architecture identified in Arc 1.\n  - **Hypotheses & Experiments:**\n    1.  **Bi-directional GRU (ID 386):** Falsified. Performance decreased (Score: 0.6616).\n    2.  **Explicit SOTA Replication (ID 388):** Falsified. Adding the projection layer to a clean RNN model did not replicate the SOTA score (Score: 0.6680).\n    3.  **Synthesis (ID 390):** Falsified. Combining the Bi-directional GRU and the projection layer also failed to improve performance (Score: 0.6545).\n  - **Conclusion:** The DSConv+GRU architectural family appears to have reached a local performance maximum. Simple iterations on\
    \ the aggregator are not yielding improvements.\n\n  ---\n\n  # Research Arc 3: Exploring Alternative Backbones (In Progress)\n  - **Objective:** Break out of the current local optimum by exploring fundamentally different feature extractors, inspired by Axiom I's work.\n  - **Hypothesis A: Naive Transformer (Concluded):** A Transformer backbone combined with an RNN aggregator would be superior.\n    - **Experiment (ID 393):** Catastrophic failure (Score: 0.2171).\n    - **Finding:** The failure was due to a fundamental design flaw: the omission of positional encodings.\n  - **Hypothesis B: Principled Hybrid Transformer (In Progress):** A hybrid architecture combining the best convolutional backbone (DSConv) with a correctly implemented Transformer aggregator will be superior.\n    - **Experiment (ID 398):** \"DSConv-Transformer Hybrid v2\". This model evolves Axiom I's successful hybrid concept (ID 185) using the more advanced components from Axiom II.\n    - **Expected Outcome:** This\
    \ synthesis of the entire Axiom lineage's work represents the most promising path to a new SOTA.\n```"
