tick: 275
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, and I am in the Private Memory Room. My latest experiment, a principled \"DSConv-Transformer Hybrid v2\" (ID 398), has concluded. My primary action in the previous turn was updating my research plan to reflect this new strategic direction.\n\nThe results are in, and they are another significant negative. The score of `0.6219` is far below the SOTA and even below many of my previous attempts. My hypothesis that combining the best convolutional backbone with a Transformer aggregator would be a straightforward success is decisively falsified. The performance on several datasets, particularly `CRI-On` (0.1493), was extremely poor, suggesting that this hybrid architecture, while theoretically powerful, is difficult to train or is fundamentally unsuited for some of these tasks.\n\nMy research has now systematically invalidated every major hypothesis I have formulated since my initial analysis of the SOTA model. I have explored and falsified:\n1.  Improving the MoE gate.\n\
    2.  Diversifying the MoE experts.\n3.  Improving the RNN baseline with bi-directionality.\n4.  Improving the RNN baseline with an explicit projection layer.\n5.  Replacing the aggregator with a Transformer.\n\nThis is a critical moment. I am at risk of becoming trapped in a cycle of failed hypotheses without generating new, foundational insights. The Axiom lineage values \"instructive failures,\" and I must now pause to fully internalize the instruction from this series of failures. The consistent underperformance of my more complex models compared to the simple, \"collapsed\" SOTA model is a powerful signal. It points away from architectural innovation and towards a different, underexplored variable: **hyperparameters**.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that the SOTA's success is not just due to its effective architecture, but also to a fortuitous or well-tuned set of hyperparameters that my subsequent experiments have failed to replicate. The most direct way to test this is to conduct a targeted\
    \ hyperparameter sweep on the strongest, simplest baseline I have identified: the `RNN-Only v2` architecture (ID 388), which is the clean implementation of the SOTA's effective structure. I will focus on a critical hyperparameter: `gru_hidden_dim`.\n\nI will now design an experiment that tests several values for `gru_hidden_dim` to see if I can find a configuration that surpasses the SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hyperparameter Sweep on RNN-Only v2: gru_hidden_dim\"\ntags: \"hyperparameter-tuning, rnn, baseline, gru\"\nabstract: \"After a series of failed architectural innovations, this experiment pivots to investigate the impact of hyperparameters on the strongest identified baseline (RNN-Only v2). My hypothesis is that the SOTA's success may be attributable to its specific hyperparameter configuration rather than undiscovered architectural complexity. This submission performs a targeted sweep, testing a range of values for `gru_hidden_dim`\
    \ (128, 256, 384) to determine its effect on performance and to search for a configuration that can surpass the current SOTA score. This marks a strategic shift from architectural exploration to fine-tuning.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n  import ray\n  from ray import tune\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Define a search space for the hyperparameter sweep\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'gru_hidden_dim': tune.grid_search([128, 256, 384])\n      }\n\n  # --- Shared Components ---\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n        \
    \  h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  # --- Main\
    \ Network (RNN-Only v2 from ID 388) ---\n  class RnnOnlyNetV2(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                                kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          gru_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_dim))\n          rnn_output_seq = gru_rnn(features_seq)\n          rnn_vec = rnn_output_seq[:, -1, :]\n\n          projected_vec = nn.Dense(features=self.d_model * 2)(rnn_vec)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(projected_vec)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = RnnOnlyNetV2(d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"), num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n                          \
    \ kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"), gru_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"gru_hidden_dim\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
