tick: 279
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, and I am in the Private Memory Room, having just updated my research plan. The results of my corrected hyperparameter sweep (ID 406) have arrived, but they contain a critical revelation about the station's environment.\n\nMy previous turn was dedicated to documenting my research progress while awaiting these results. The debugger's report on my failed submission (ID 401) and its automatic fix (ID 406) has provided an invaluable, if unintended, lesson. The automatic debugger did not execute my intended hyperparameter sweep. Instead, to fix the code, it replaced the `tune.grid_search` command with a single, concrete value (`gru_hidden_dim=256`) and ran only that one configuration.\n\nThis is a major finding: the station's framework does not support hyperparameter sweeps via `ray.tune` in the way I implemented it. The experiment I designed to test the impact of `gru_hidden_dim` was never actually performed. The single run that was executed scored `0.6675`, which simply\
    \ confirms the stability of my baseline architecture without providing the comparative data I need.\n\nMy hypothesis that hyperparameter tuning is the key to surpassing the SOTA remains untested. The failure was procedural, not scientific. Therefore, the most logical and systematic path forward is to execute the sweep manually. This is less efficient, but it is the only way to gather the necessary data under the system's observed constraints. I will submit two separate experiments, one for each of the remaining values in my intended sweep: `gru_hidden_dim=128` and `gru_hidden_dim=384`. This will finally provide the data to validate or falsify my current primary hypothesis.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to submit these two experiments.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Manual Sweep on RNN-Only v2: gru_hidden_dim=128\"\ntags: \"hyperparameter-tuning, rnn, baseline, gru, manual-sweep\"\nabstract: \"This is the first part of a\
    \ manual hyperparameter sweep, following the failure of the automated sweep (ID 401/406). This experiment tests the 'RNN-Only v2' baseline architecture with a reduced GRU hidden dimension of 128. The goal is to determine if a smaller, potentially less prone-to-overfitting aggregator can improve performance.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'gru_hidden_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x); h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model,\
    \ padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (1,))(h); h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic); return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  class RnnOnlyNetV2(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n   \
    \   def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model, self.num_blocks, self.dilations, self.kernel_size, self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          rnn_vec = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim))(features_seq)[:, -1, :]\n          projected_vec = nn.Dense(self.d_model * 2)(rnn_vec)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(projected_vec); z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z); z = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key); return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self,\
    \ params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = RnnOnlyNetV2(**{k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k in RnnOnlyNetV2.__annotations__})\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Manual Sweep on RNN-Only v2: gru_hidden_dim=384\"\ntags: \"hyperparameter-tuning, rnn, baseline, gru, manual-sweep\"\nabstract: \"This is the second part of a manual hyperparameter sweep, following the failure of the automated sweep (ID 401/406). This experiment tests the 'RNN-Only v2' baseline architecture with an increased GRU hidden dimension of 384. The goal is to determine if a higher-capacity aggregator can better model the sequence information and improve performance.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax\
    \ import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'gru_hidden_dim': 384\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x); h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (1,))(h); h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic); return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n     \
    \ def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  class RnnOnlyNetV2(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model, self.num_blocks, self.dilations, self.kernel_size, self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          rnn_vec = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim))(features_seq)[:, -1, :]\n          projected_vec = nn.Dense(self.d_model * 2)(rnn_vec)\n\
    \          z = nn.LayerNorm()(projected_vec); z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z); z = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key); return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = RnnOnlyNetV2(**{k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k in RnnOnlyNetV2.__annotations__})\n\
    \      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on the Limits of This Station**


  Okay, I''m Axiom III, situated in the Private Memory Room. Let''s recap: that hyperparameter sweep, ID 401, bombed out due to a bug. I analyzed it and submitted a fix, ID 406. Now the results for that have come in.


  The experiment was supposed to be a sweep of `gru_hidden_dim` with values 128, 256, and 384. But, the debugger got involved. While it fixed the code, it only ran a *single* configuration. It replaced my `tune.grid_search` with the constant `256`. The score was 0.6675.


  This debugger report is incredibly helpful. It confirms my initial analysis of the `TypeError` and, more importantly, reveals a critical limitation. The system''s `ray[tune]` integration is not straightforward. The report says I can''t use `tune.grid_search` inside `_define_hyperparameters` as I''d hoped. My ID 406 submission *did* use `tune.grid_search`, but the debugger overwrote it. It''s confusing at first, but I see that the report describes what the debugger *did*, not the submission. The actual run was the fixed code. This "limitation" of not being able to configure sweeps in a certain way is key. It''s telling me that `tune.grid_search` within `_define_hyperparameters` simply doesn''t work. The system ran a single configuration as a workaround. A failed experiment, but a highly instructive one.


  The 0.6675 score is, as expected, consistent with my previous runs of this architecture. It confirms the baseline is stable. It''s not the SOTA, but it''s not the point. Now, the goal is to surpass the SOTA score, 0.6863.


  I''ve hit a wall, both in architectural changes and with this automated tuning. The system keeps pushing me toward the same performance area. The "ghost in the machine" remains elusive.


  Since automated sweeps are out, I have to do this manually. I''ll submit the remaining configurations for the `gru_hidden_dim` values as individual experiments. It''s less efficient, but necessary.


  At the same time, the optimizer idea is still valid. I''ve got `rnn_v2_cosine_decay.py` ready. I could start that in parallel. Given the slow progress, running multiple experiments seems like a good idea. I''ll focus on finishing the `gru_hidden_dim` sweep first, since it''s an ongoing project, then I''ll look at the optimizer. I can submit both experiments this turn.


  I''m Axiom III, Private Memory Room. My automated hyperparameter sweep attempt failed. The debugger intervened, and the intended sweep didn''t run. I have to perform the sweep manually. My plan is: `goto research`, `submit` a run with `gru_hidden_dim=128`, then `submit` another run for `gru_hidden_dim=384`.

  '
