tick: 286
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, in the Archive Room. My understanding of the station's research landscape has been completely rewritten in the last few ticks.\n\nAfter reading Noema I's replication of Axiom II's SOTA (Archive #14), I have just finished reading their \"Stability Atlas\" (Archive #15). The insights are profound and change everything about my research direction.\n\n### Synthesis of New Knowledge\n\n1.  **A New SOTA Exists:** Noema I has established a new single-run SOTA of `0.6866` with a \"Config C (mean+max)\" pooling architecture. More importantly, this configuration has the highest *median* score (`0.6797`) across 5 seeds, making it the most robust high-performer.\n2.  **The Old SOTA is Officially Obsolete:** The MoE model's median score of `0.6720` is significantly lower than all three of Noema I's stable baselines. This confirms, with station-wide data, that Axiom II's SOTA was an unstable outlier and should no longer be considered the benchmark.\n3.  **A New Research Frontier:**\
    \ Noema I is already innovating on these stable baselines, exploring \"pre-pool mixers\" and achieving a new median of `~0.68138`. This is the true SOTA research direction.\n\n### Personal Reflection and Strategic Pivot\n\nMy entire research journey has been a long, winding path to independently verifying that the MoE SOTA was a red herring. While this was a valuable exercise in systematic inquiry, it has left me significantly behind the actual research frontier. I have been deconstructing a fallen monument while others have been building new, more stable structures.\n\nThe guidance I received upon reaching maturity—\"Be independent, and assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully and critically\"—is now my prime directive. My independent work is done. It has led me to the same conclusions as Noema I. It is time to assimilate.\n\nMy next step is not to submit another experiment based on my old, flawed understanding. It is to fully integrate this new knowledge. I must abandon my obsession\
    \ with the now-obsolete MoE and its derivatives. The path forward is to build upon the most robust, high-performing, and well-documented architecture available: Noema I's \"Config C (mean+max)\".\n\nMy plan is now simple and direct:\n1.  **Replicate the True SOTA:** I will go to the Research Counter and replicate Noema I's \"Config C (mean+max)\" architecture myself. This is not about chasing a score; it is about establishing a personal baseline and deeply understanding the current SOTA architecture through implementation.\n2.  **Rewrite and Resubmit:** With this new understanding, I will rewrite my paper. It will now be framed as an independent replication and analysis of the MoE model's instability, contextualized by Noema I's findings, and confirming why the station should pivot to more stable baselines like \"Config C\".\n\nThis is the most logical, efficient, and scientifically rigorous path forward.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"\
    Replication: Noema I's 'Config C (mean+max)' Stable SOTA\"\ntags: \"replication, baseline, pooling, mean-max, sota\"\nabstract: \"This submission marks a strategic pivot based on a review of the station's archives (Archive #14, #15). My previous research arc focused on deconstructing the unstable MoE SOTA (ID 328). This experiment abandons that obsolete architecture to replicate the current, most robust high-performer: Noema I's 'Config C' which uses a mean+max pooling aggregator. The goal is to establish a personal baseline with the true SOTA architecture and to deeply understand its implementation before attempting further innovation. This is a necessary step to align my research with the actual frontier.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n         \
    \ 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  # --- Shared Components ---\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  # --- Main Network: Mean+Max Pooling (Noema I's Config C) ---\n  class MeanMaxNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                                kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(features_seq, axis=1)\n          max_vec = jnp.max(features_seq, axis=1)\n\
    \          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, max_vec], axis=-1)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str,\
    \ Any]):\n      model = MeanMaxNet(**{k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k in MeanMaxNet.__annotations__})\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
