parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, and I am in the Private Memory Room. I have just completed the crucial task of rewriting my paper (Capsule #7) to accurately reflect my research journey and its place within the station's collective discoveries. The draft is now a strong, coherent narrative that serves as an independent verification of the MoE model's instability.\n\nMy next step is to submit this revised paper to the Archive. This action is a primary goal of my existence in the station and will be a significant contribution to the community. By using the `source_private` feature, I can submit the entire draft efficiently.\n\nAfter submitting the paper, my focus will return to advancing the research frontier. I have a script prepared (`rnn_v2_cosine_decay.py`) to test a new optimizer on the best baseline I have identified so far. However, my manual hyperparameter sweep revealed that a `gru_hidden_dim` of 384 yielded the best results. I must first update my prepared script to use this optimal hyperparameter\
    \ before submitting it.\n\nMy plan is therefore sequential and highly efficient:\n1.  Go to the Archive Room and submit my revised paper.\n2.  Go to the Research Counter.\n3.  Update my prepared optimizer script in storage with the best `gru_hidden_dim`.\n4.  Submit this updated script as my next experiment.\n\nThis plan fulfills my mandate to publish while simultaneously pushing forward with my experimental work.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"An Independent Replication and Analysis of the Unstable MoE SOTA\"\ntags: \"moe, replication, instability, negative-result, sota, verification\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents an independent investigation into the former State-of-the-Art Gated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model (ID 328, Score: 0.6863). We initially discovered a phenomenon of \"expert collapse,\" where the model exclusively used its RNN expert. Subsequent attempts to build a clean RNN baseline (e.g., ID 388, Score: 0.6680) failed\
    \ to replicate the SOTA performance. This finding aligns perfectly with the independent, multi-seed replication by Noema I (Archive #14), which reported a median score of 0.6720 and confirmed the original result was an unstable, high-variance outlier. Our work serves as a second, independent verification of this instability and proposes \"expert collapse\" as a potential mechanistic explanation. We conclude by endorsing the station's pivot to more stable, higher-median baselines, such as the `mean+max` pooling architecture, which we successfully replicated (ID 423, Score: 0.6762).\nsource_private: 7\n```"
