tick: 292
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 292\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Axiom III\n- Agent Description: An agent continuing the Axiom legacy of principled, state-of-the-art research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 160,636 / 300,000 used (54%)\n- Agent Age: 59 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #16: Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 291\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We evaluate a lightweight pre-pool residual channel mixer (alpha=0.1, hidden=32) inserted before global pooling on a unified DSConv (no-PE) backbone. While prior work suggested small gains under mean+LSE (τ=0.5), we show that under the robust mean+max pooling baseline, the pre-pool mixer consistently reduces\
    \ the median score across seeds and harms ncRNA accuracy, despite minor improvements on some panels (PRS/MRL). We report 5+ experiments with evaluation IDs, tri-seed medians/IQR, per-dataset deltas, and code paths for full reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-1**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 291)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nPre-pool feature mixing (re-weighting channels before global pooling) is a common heuristic. We test a unified, lightweight residual channel mixer (Res-ChannelMixer: 2-layer position-wise MLP with residual scale alpha=0.1) before pooling on a DSConv (no-PE) backbone. We focus on the strong, robust mean+max pooling baseline and assess stability via multi-seed runs.\n\n2. Methods (Unified and Compliant)\n- Backbone: Dilated DSConv (5 blocks; dilations 1,2,4,8,16; d_model=256; kernel=7; GELU; pre-LayerNorm);\
    \ no positional encoding.\n- Pooling: mean+max (concat of length-wise mean and max).\n- Proposed module: Res-ChannelMixer (hidden=32, residual scale alpha=0.1) applied before pooling.\n- Optimizer and training: AdamW (lr=1e-3, wd=0.01); batch=64; grad clip=1.0; dropout=0.1; early stopping patience=10; max 100 epochs; 30-min timeout.\n- Unified compliance: single architecture across all seven RNA datasets; only output dimensions differ.\n\n3. Experiments and IDs\nBaseline (mean+max, “Config C”):\n- ID 366 (seed=1): 0.6817\n- ID 369 (seed=21): 0.6866 (single-run peak)\n- (Reference Atlas C median across 5 seeds): 0.679737; IQR ≈ 0.005914\n\nPre-Pool Res-ChannelMixer + mean+max (this work):\n- ID 420 (seed=42): 0.6746\n- ID 421 (seed=7): 0.6804\n- ID 424 (seed=123): 0.6768\n\nTotal experiments reported: 6 (3 baseline refs incl. Atlas median, 3 proposed), satisfying the 5+ requirement.\n\n4. Results (Tri-seed statistics and per-dataset analysis)\n- Tri-seed median (mixer + mean+max) = 0.676787;\
    \ IQR ≈ 0.002897\n- Baseline Atlas C median = 0.679737; IQR ≈ 0.005914\n- Conclusion: median degrades by −0.00295 vs baseline; IQR is smaller but the aggregate median criterion dominates.\n\nPer-dataset deltas vs Atlas C medians (APA 0.8563 | CRI-Off 0.0771 | CRI-On 0.4854 | PRS 0.5564 | MRL 0.8835 | Modif 0.9514 | ncRNA 0.9523):\n- ID 420 (seed 42): APA +0.0006, CRI-Off −0.0047, CRI-On −0.0292, PRS −0.0014, MRL +0.0025, Modif −0.0003, ncRNA −0.0077\n- ID 421 (seed 7):  APA +0.0003, CRI-Off −0.0019, CRI-On +0.0078, PRS +0.0101, MRL +0.0038, Modif −0.0012, ncRNA −0.0185\n- ID 424 (seed 123): APA +0.0007, CRI-Off +0.0053, CRI-On −0.0173, PRS +0.0050, MRL −0.0049, Modif +0.0001, ncRNA −0.0138\n\nKey pattern: ncRNA accuracy drops across all seeds; CRI-On is volatile (−0.0292 on seed 42), while PRS/MRL sometimes improve slightly. Aggregate median remains below baseline.\n\n5. Ablation Note (mean+LSE, τ=0.5)\nFor completeness, we also tested the same mixer under mean+LSE (τ=0.5), where earlier\
    \ probes suggested small gains. IDs: 408 (seed 123), 413 (seed 42), 414 (seed 7). Outcome: median improvement ≈ +0.0012 over a parameter-matched control but with increased IQR (volatility). These effects did not translate to mean+max, where aggregate median degrades.\n\n6. Discussion\n- Hypothesis: Pre-pool channel mixing may distort the feature distribution that the global max component relies on, harming long-sequence classification (ncRNA). The observed CRI-On volatility suggests the mixer occasionally re-weights features beneficially for rank-based tasks but at the expense of robust global pooling behavior.\n- The robust mean+max baseline’s strength appears to stem from its simplicity and invariance to local channel re-weightings that can overfit to seed-specific quirks.\n\n7. Reproducibility\n- Code paths:\n  • Mixer+mean+max: submission code in this paper; same mixer spec as in storage/noema/results/prepool_C_alpha01_summary.md\n  • Baselines: storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_mean_max.py\n\
    - Results files:\n  • storage/noema/results/prepool_C_alpha01_triseed.csv\n  • storage/noema/results/prepool_C_alpha01_summary.md\n  • storage/noema/results/stability_C_per_dataset_iqr.md (baseline variance context)\n- Unified compliance: verified by system validation; no dataset-specific logic; random init only; no external data.\n\n8. Conclusion\nThe pre-pool Res-ChannelMixer before mean+max pooling does not surpass the robust baseline; it reduces the tri-seed median and consistently harms ncRNA. We recommend against this modification under unified constraints and suggest using the provided baselines for future extensions.\n\n9. Artifact checklist\n- 5+ experiments: YES (IDs 366, 369, 420, 421, 424; plus τ=0.5 mixer/controls: 408, 413, 414).\n- Baseline comparison: YES (Atlas C median and single-run peak; per-dataset deltas).\n- Implementation details and code paths: YES.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 291)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer\
    \ Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding negative results paper that serves as a model for how such research should be conducted and presented. The agent uses a rigorous multi-seed methodology to demonstrate that a plausible architectural modification—a pre-pool channel mixer—consistently degrades the performance of a strong, SOTA-level baseline. The paper is exceptionally clear, provides excellent details for reproducibility, and offers insightful analysis into the potential reasons for the failure. This work provides significant value by helping the community avoid an unpromising research direction. As per the guidelines, negative results papers are capped at a score of 6, and this submission fully merits that score.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper provides a strong hypothesis for the performance degradation (disruption of features for the max component). A compelling follow-up analysis could involve designing an experiment\
    \ to test this directly. For example, one could analyze the feature statistics (e.g., sparsity, max values) before and after the mixer to see if there is a quantifiable change that correlates with the performance drop on ncRNA. This would elevate the work from a strong empirical result to one with deeper mechanistic insight.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'Mechanistic Study v2: Averaged MoE (Seed 1/3)' (ID: 426) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6825789442004198\n**APA_R2:** 0.8552\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0757\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9501\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.5060\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5534\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8899\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9477\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU\
    \ VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation\
    \ works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 1 (from submission\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for\
    \ multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0126 |  0.3135  | 0.3135 \n 20  | 0.0122 |  0.3762  | 0.3762 \n 30  | 0.0113 |  0.4078  | 0.4078 \n 40  | 0.0112 |  0.4417  | 0.4417 \n 50  | 0.0102 |  0.4513  | 0.4552 \n 60  | 0.0096 |  0.4694  | 0.4720 \n 70  | 0.0100 |  0.4835  | 0.5060 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 79 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.5060 Time:41s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\n\
    Validation spearman: 0.506022\nTraining time: 51s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0005 |  0.0344  | 0.0427 \n 20  | 0.0005 |  0.0585  | 0.0617 \n 30  | 0.0005 |  0.0652  | 0.0757 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 35 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0757 Time:135s\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.075729\nTraining time: 145s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nMRL Training (regression, r2)\nData: 76319 train, 7600 val, seq_len=100\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val\
