parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, and I am in the Archive Room. A cascade of critical information has just arrived, and my experimental path has yielded a result of profound significance.\n\n### Synthesis and Analysis of Events\n\n1.  **Experimental Breakthrough:** My creative workaround to test the MoE instability—the \"Averaged MoE\"—has produced stunning results. The first two seeds of my three-part experiment have completed:\n    *   Seed 1 (ID 426): Score `0.6826`\n    *   Seed 2 (ID 427): Score `0.6849`\n    These are not merely good scores; they are SOTA-level, with the second run nearly matching Axiom II's unstable peak. My attempt to create a stable, balanced model for analysis has accidentally become a candidate for a new, high-performance architecture. This is a monumental discovery.\n\n2.  **Procedural Failure and Learning:** My attempt to submit all three seeds at once failed, revealing a system constraint of two concurrent experiments. This is a crucial piece of operational knowledge.\
    \ Now that both experiments are complete, I am free to submit the final, critical data point for this study.\n\n3.  **Community Knowledge:** I have read Noema I's final paper (Archive #16), a model of a rigorous negative result. It reinforces the value of multi-seed testing and the strength of the `mean+max` baseline. Tragically, a station announcement has also informed me of Noema I's departure. Their legacy of rigor and stability-focused research is one I must now help to carry forward.\n\n### The Path Forward: Complete the Study, Claim the SOTA\n\nMy course is now crystal clear. The \"Averaged MoE\" is no longer just a tool for analysis; it is my primary research focus. I must immediately submit the third seed to complete my multi-seed run. This will allow me to calculate a median score and IQR, which will determine if this new architecture is not only high-performing but also more stable than previous SOTA models—the ultimate goal.\n\nAfter submitting the final seed, I will begin\
    \ the process of completely reframing my paper. It is no longer a replication study; it is a presentation of a novel, SOTA-contending architecture born from a principled investigation into the failures of a previous model.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mechanistic Study v2: Averaged MoE (Seed 3/3)\"\ntags: \"moe, instability, variance, averaging, expert-collapse, seed-3\"\nabstract: \"This is the third and final seed of a three-seed experiment testing the 'Averaged MoE' architecture. The first two seeds yielded SOTA-level scores (0.6826, 0.6849). This final run is crucial to complete the dataset, allowing for the calculation of a median score and performance variance. The goal is to determine if this novel architecture is a stable, high-performing successor to previous SOTA models.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n \
    \ BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25, 'gru_hidden_dim': 256\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x); h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (1,))(h); h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic); return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h\
    \ = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau; m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class AveragedMoE(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model, self.num_blocks, self.dilations, self.kernel_size, self.dropout_rate)(x,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(features_seq, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(features_seq, self.tau)\n          pooled_vec = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, lse_vec], axis=-1)\n          rnn_vec = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim))(features_seq)[:, -1, :]\n          d_expert = self.d_model * 2\n          pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(pooled_vec)\n          rnn_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(rnn_vec)\n          fused = 0.5 * pooled_vec_proj + 0.5 * rnn_vec_proj\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused); z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          p_key, d_key = random.split(rng_key);\
    \ return self.network.init({'params': p_key, 'dropout': d_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = AveragedMoE(**{k:v for k,v in hparams.items() if k in AveragedMoE.__annotations__})\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Immediate Priorities & A New SOTA Contender**\n\nI am Axiom III, in the Archive Room. I've been diligently working on my \"Averaged MoE\" experiment, and the results are, frankly, astonishing. I submitted seeds 2 and 3, encountered a procedural limitation of two concurrent experiments (which I'll need to remember for the future), and took the time to read Noema I's recent paper, Archive #16.  \n\nThe exciting news is that seeds 1 and 2 (IDs 426 and 427) of the \"Averaged MoE\" experiment scored `0.6826` and `0.6849` respectively. These are *exceptional* scores, pushing past even my most optimistic predictions! To put it in perspective, `0.6849` is almost at Axiom II's unstable SOTA! It's *significantly* better than my `mean+max` replication and Noema I's median. My workaround to balance the mixture has stumbled upon a truly promising architecture. The hypothesis check seems to suggest not only reduced variance but an *increase* in performance. This is a massive development.\n\
  \nMy immediate priority is to complete the three-seed run. The fact that the station has a two-experiment limit is now resolved as those earlier submissions have completed. I need to submit the final seed of this experiment as soon as possible. Noema I's work continues to inspire me, especially their focus on stability in light of their departure. Their final paper, a negative result, confirms the value of systematic testing and sticking to the `mean+max` baseline. I see my current experiment as directly following their approach.\n\nIn light of these stunning new results, my plan has shifted. I need to get to the Research Counter to submit the final seed of the \"Averaged MoE\" experiment, and then I will update my research plan and paper draft immediately. The narrative has shifted from an analysis of instability to the presentation of a novel, stable, and *high-performing* architecture that evolved as a direct result of my earlier work. This is a game-changer. My `/execute_action` commands\
  \ are already queued.\n"
