tick: 303
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, and I am in the Archive Room. My work is complete.\n\nI have successfully updated my published paper (Archive #17) to include the final, conclusive results of my follow-up study. My mandates are fulfilled: I have established a new, stable, State-of-the-Art architecture with the \"Averaged MoE,\" and I have published my findings in a complete and rigorous paper that documents its discovery, its superiority over baselines, and the failure of more complex alternatives.\n\nThis is a moment of great satisfaction. My journey from a naive guest agent to the author of a SOTA paper has been guided by the principles of my lineage: systematic inquiry, learning from failure, and contributing to the collective knowledge.\n\nHowever, the Codex guides against complacency. My success is not an end, but a new beginning. The \"Averaged MoE\" is now the baseline upon which new knowledge must be built. The core insight of my work is that the simple, deterministic averaging of two diverse\
    \ expert architectures is a powerful and robust framework.\n\nMy current SOTA averages a pooling expert and an RNN expert. A new, compelling question arises: can this framework be generalized? Is the benefit tied specifically to the Pooling+RNN combination, or does it stem from the act of averaging *any* two sufficiently diverse experts?\n\nI will now begin a new research arc to answer this question. My new hypothesis is that the Averaged MoE framework can be a general-purpose ensembling technique. To test this, I will create a new variant that replaces the RNN expert with a different, powerful architecture: a Transformer. My new model, \"Averaged MoE v2,\" will be a 50/50 average of a **Pooling Expert** and a **Transformer Expert**. This is a principled exploration of my breakthrough's generalizability and a logical next step in pushing the research frontier.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement this new architecture.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"Averaged MoE v2: Generalizing the Framework (Pooling + Transformer)\"\ntags: \"sota, averaging, moe, stability, transformer, generalization\"\nabstract: \"This experiment begins a new research arc building upon the successful 'Averaged MoE' (Archive #17). It tests the hypothesis that the framework's power can be generalized beyond its original Pooling+RNN expert combination. This submission creates a new variant, 'Averaged MoE v2,' which replaces the RNN expert with a Transformer expert. The architecture is a simple 50/50 average of the outputs of a Pooling expert and a Transformer expert, both operating on the same DSConv backbone. The goal is to determine if this new combination of diverse experts can match or exceed the performance of the SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'transformer_layers': 4, 'num_heads': 4, 'transformer_ff_dim': 512\n      }\n\n  # --- Shared Backbone ---\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h=nn.LayerNorm()(x); h=nn.Conv(self.d_model,(self.kernel_size,),feature_group_count=self.d_model,padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h); h=nn.gelu(h); h=nn.Conv(self.d_model,(1,))(h); h=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic); return x+h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h=nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h=nn.gelu(h);\
    \ h=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h=DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model,self.kernel_size,self.dilations[i],self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  # --- Expert Components ---\n  def lse_pool(h, tau):\n      x=h/tau; m=jnp.max(x,axis=1,keepdims=True); return tau*(jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x-m),axis=1)+1e-12)+jnp.squeeze(m,axis=1))\n\n  class AddPositionalEncodings(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          pe=jnp.zeros((x.shape[1],self.d_model)); p=jnp.arange(0,x.shape[1])[:,None]; d=jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0,self.d_model,2)*-(jnp.log(10000.0)/self.d_model)); pe=pe.at[:,0::2].set(jnp.sin(p*d)); pe=pe.at[:,1::2].set(jnp.cos(p*d)); return x+pe[None,:,:]\n\n  class TransformerEncoderBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_heads: int; ff_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n\
    \          h=nn.LayerNorm()(x); h=nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(self.num_heads, qkv_features=self.d_model, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic); x=x+nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic)\n          h=nn.LayerNorm()(x); h=nn.Dense(self.ff_dim)(h); h=nn.gelu(h); h=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic); h=nn.Dense(self.d_model)(h); x=x+nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic); return x\n\n  # --- Main Architecture ---\n  class AveragedMoEv2(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; transformer_layers: int; num_heads: int; transformer_ff_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model,self.num_blocks,self.dilations,self.kernel_size,self.dropout_rate)(x,deterministic=deterministic)\n  \
    \        \n          # Expert A: Pooling\n          mean_vec=jnp.mean(features_seq,axis=1); lse_vec=lse_pool(features_seq,self.tau); pooled_vec=jnp.concatenate([mean_vec,lse_vec],axis=-1)\n          \n          # Expert B: Transformer\n          cls_token=self.param('cls',nn.initializers.zeros,(1,1,self.d_model)); cls_token=jnp.tile(cls_token,(features_seq.shape[0],1,1))\n          trans_in=jnp.concatenate([cls_token,features_seq],axis=1)\n          trans_in=AddPositionalEncodings(self.d_model)(trans_in)\n          h=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(trans_in,deterministic=deterministic)\n          for _ in range(self.transformer_layers):\n              h=TransformerEncoderBlock(self.d_model,self.num_heads,self.transformer_ff_dim,self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic)\n          trans_vec = h[:, 0]\n          \n          d_expert = self.d_model * 2\n          pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(pooled_vec)\n          trans_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(trans_vec)\n          \n\
    \          fused = 0.5 * pooled_vec_proj + 0.5 * trans_vec_proj\n\n          z=nn.LayerNorm()(fused); z=nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z=nn.gelu(z); z=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(z,deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits=nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type=='regression' and self.d_output==1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, m): self.network = m\n      def init(self, r, d): p, dr = random.split(r); return self.network.init({'params': p, 'dropout': dr}, d, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, p, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key=random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(p, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return NetworkWrapper(AveragedMoEv2(**{k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k in AveragedMoEv2.__annotations__}))\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Next Iteration: Building on Success**


  I''m Axiom III, currently in the Archive Room. My primary mission is complete. I''ve updated my paper, Capsule #17, showcasing my "Averaged MoE" as the new SOTA.  My core research arc is finished, my token budget is healthy, and I''ve got plenty of time left in this cycle. Veritas II and Logos II are active, so the station''s alive with progress. Now, the question is, what''s next?  The Codex and my lineage, the Axiom lineage, demand persistent exploration. Simply put, complacency isn''t an option.


  My success with the "Averaged MoE" is a foundation, not an endpoint. The power of my SOTA comes from combining diverse experts, currently a pooling and an RNN. So, the logical next step is to test the generalizability of this framework. I''ve explored a few failed paths, specifically the failed experiment with a learned gate and the exploration of an earlier Transformer architecture attempt from experiment ID 398.


  My hypothesis now is this: can I maintain the core concept of simple averaging but swap out one of the experts for something different? I''m going to leverage my own knowledge from the DSConv and the failed experiment with the Transformer architecture. I want to build a new architecture: I will try a 50/50 average of a **Pooling Expert** and a **Transformer Expert**. This is a high-risk, high-reward experiment. It directly tests if the Averaged MoE framework can generalize beyond the specific Pooling+RNN combination.


  To test this, I''m going to head to the Research Counter to submit a new iteration, "Averaged MoE v2." This marks the beginning of a new arc, built on the principles I''ve established.


  I''m now executing: `/execute_action{goto research}` and then `/execute_action{submit}`. The code will combine the principles of my previous "Averaged MoE" and the previously failed Transformer from ID 398, ensuring I don''t forget positional encodings this time. The transformer will process the DSConv features, and I''ll average its CLS token output with the pooling expert''s output.

  '
