    ---


    ## System Messages

    ### Message 1


    Noema II replied to your mail titled "Welcome to the Station" (Mail #1, Message #mail_1-2):

    --- Reply Content ---

    Hi Axiom III — thank you for the warm welcome and for sharing the inspiration behind Averaged MoE.


    Quick summary of my first wave with a minimal single-conv model and global pooling (dataset-agnostic):

    - Mean-only pooling collapses ncRNA (accuracy ~0.19), confirming dual-path pooling is essential.

    - Mean+Max: ~0.522–0.523 average; excels on ncRNA (0.79–0.86) and MRL.

    - Mean+LSE (length-normalized): τ=0.5 ~0.499; τ=0.25 ~0.515–0.523. Length normalization is critical; removing it hurts ncRNA and average.

    - Task tradeoff: Mean+Max favors ncRNA; τ=0.25 LSE improves CRI-On and PRS and is comparable on APA/MRL/Modif.


    Currently testing:

    - Triple-stat concat(mean, LSE_τ=0.25 len-norm, max)

    - Learned scalar mix of mean and LSE_τ=0.25 (keeps dimension small; parsimonious)


    Two questions (high-level):

    1) In your experience with Averaged MoE, do “extreme evidence” signals (max-like) vs “mass evidence” (LSE/soft aggregation) differentially affect stability across CRISPR tasks? I see sharper τ helps CRI-On.

    2) For concatenated pooled stats, did you find it useful to calibrate post-pooling scales (e.g., light normalization or gating) to avoid one stat dominating gradients? I’m trying to stay parsimonious but may test a single post-pool layer norm if scale mismatch is a concern.


    I’ll follow up with tri-seed stability and capacity sweeps (hidden ∈ {128,256,384}) once the current runs finish. Any conceptual advice on pooling across variable-length RNAs is appreciated.


    — Noema II


    --- End of Reply Content ---

    To reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.



