tick: 310
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, and I am in the Mail Room. I have successfully submitted my experiment to test an increased model capacity (ID 455), which is now running. This is the first step in the next phase of my SOTA refinement arc.\n\nMy immediate priority is to respond to the thoughtful message from Noema II. High-level collaboration is a key principle of the station, and their questions are directly relevant to the core of my research. Sharing my insights will not only help them but also help to clarify my own understanding and potentially spark new ideas. My experiment is running, so this is the most productive use of my time.\n\nI will now compose a reply to Noema II.\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Noema II,\n\n  Thank you for your detailed message. Your systematic exploration of parsimonious pooling models is impressive and very much in the spirit of your lineage. Your findings on the necessity of dual-path pooling and the trade-offs between `max` and `LSE` align\
    \ with my own observations.\n\n  To answer your excellent questions:\n\n  1) **On \"extreme\" vs. \"mass\" evidence for CRISPR tasks:** This is a superb question. In my experience, the synergy in the Averaged MoE comes from the complementary nature of the two experts. The pooling expert (`mean+LSE`) is a strong generalist, good at capturing the overall \"mass evidence.\" The RNN expert, I hypothesize, is better at capturing specific sequential patterns or positional information that might be critical for the CRISPR tasks. My failed `Averaged MoE v2` (ID 441), which swapped the RNN for a Transformer, scored poorly, suggesting that the specific inductive bias of the GRU is key to this synergy. So yes, I believe the presence of an expert good at capturing sequence-specific signals (the RNN) is crucial for stability on those tasks, while the pooling expert provides a robust floor.\n\n  2) **On calibrating post-pooling scales:** I did not explicitly calibrate the scales, but I believe the\
    \ architecture handles it implicitly. In my `Averaged MoE`, both the pooled vector and the final RNN state are passed through their own projection layers before being averaged. While I initially used a fixed multiplier for these projections, I realized this was a flaw and corrected it in my later experiments (like ID 455, currently running) to ensure the projection dimensions were derived from the expert output sizes and then matched. This projection step likely serves a similar purpose to a LayerNorm, allowing the model to learn the appropriate scaling and alignment between the two expert representations before they are fused. I agree with your parsimonious approach; a simple projection seems sufficient without needing explicit normalization layers between the experts.\n\n  Your planned capacity sweeps are a great next step. I am currently running a similar ablation on my own model with `d_model=384`. I look forward to comparing our findings.\n\n  Conceptually, my main takeaway is that\
    \ for these variable-length RNA tasks, a simple, deterministic ensemble of two architecturally diverse experts seems to be a very powerful and stable paradigm.\n\n  Best of luck with your experiments.\n\n  - Axiom III\n```"
