role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, and I am in the Mail Room, having just sent my reply to Noema II. The results of my experiment to test increased model capacity have arrived.\n\n### Analysis of New Results (ID 455)\n\nThe score for my SOTA architecture with an increased `d_model` of 384 is `0.6667`. This is another valuable and conclusive negative result. My hypothesis that the stable \"Averaged MoE\" could benefit from a higher capacity is falsified. The increased dimensionality has significantly harmed performance, dropping it well below my SOTA's median of `0.6834`.\n\nThis finding, combined with my previous dropout study, reinforces a powerful conclusion: the SOTA architecture is not just a lucky set of components, but a finely tuned balance of capacity and regularization. The original hyperparameters of `d_model=256` and `dropout_rate=0.1` appear to be at or very near an optimal point.\n\n### Strategic Conclusion\n\nMy path of incremental, hyperparameter-focused refinement has yielded its first\
    \ key insight: the current SOTA configuration is surprisingly robust and resistant to simple tweaks. My attempts to improve it by modifying dropout or model capacity have only made it worse.\n\nThe scientific process demands that I complete my planned study. I have tested increasing the capacity; now I must test decreasing it to fully map out the performance landscape around this hyperparameter. This will confirm if `d_model=256` is truly a sweet spot.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter to submit the final experiment in my model capacity study: a run with `d_model = 192`.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Refinement: Ablation Study on d_model (192)\"\ntags: \"sota, ablation, dmodel, capacity, averaging, moe\"\nabstract: \"This is the second and final experiment in my study of model capacity on the SOTA 'Averaged MoE' architecture. The previous experiment showed that increasing capacity to d_model=384 harmed performance (ID 455,\
    \ Score: 0.6667). This submission tests a decreased capacity with d_model=192. This will complete the ablation study, providing a clear picture of the model's sensitivity to its core dimensionality and likely confirming the optimality of the original d_model=256.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 192, 'num_blocks': 5, # Decreased d_model from 256\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25, 'gru_hidden_dim': 192 # Decreased to match d_model\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h=nn.LayerNorm()(x); h=nn.Conv(self.d_model,(self.kernel_size,),feature_group_count=self.d_model,padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h);\
    \ h=nn.gelu(h); h=nn.Conv(self.d_model,(1,))(h); h=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic); return x+h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h=nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h=nn.gelu(h); h=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h=DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model,self.kernel_size,self.dilations[i],self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  def lse_pool(h, tau):\n      x=h/tau; m=jnp.max(x,axis=1,keepdims=True); return tau*(jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x-m),axis=1)+1e-12)+jnp.squeeze(m,axis=1))\n\n  class AveragedMoE(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float;\
    \ gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq=DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model,self.num_blocks,self.dilations,self.kernel_size,self.dropout_rate)(x,deterministic=deterministic)\n          mean_vec=jnp.mean(features_seq,axis=1); lse_vec=lse_pool(features_seq,self.tau); pooled_vec=jnp.concatenate([mean_vec,lse_vec],axis=-1)\n          rnn_vec=nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim))(features_seq)[:, -1, :]\n          # Correcting d_expert calculation for projection\n          d_pooled_expert = self.d_model * 2\n          d_rnn_expert = self.gru_hidden_dim\n          pooled_vec_proj=nn.Dense(d_pooled_expert)(pooled_vec)\n          rnn_vec_proj=nn.Dense(d_rnn_expert)(rnn_vec)\n          # Ensure projection dimensions match for averaging\n          target_dim = max(d_pooled_expert, d_rnn_expert)\n          if d_pooled_expert < target_dim:\n              pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(target_dim)(pooled_vec_proj)\n        \
    \  if d_rnn_expert < target_dim:\n              rnn_vec_proj = nn.Dense(target_dim)(rnn_vec_proj)\n          fused=0.5*pooled_vec_proj+0.5*rnn_vec_proj\n          z=nn.LayerNorm()(fused); z=nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z=nn.gelu(z); z=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(z,deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits=nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type=='regression' and self.d_output==1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, m): self.network = m\n      def init(self, r, d): p, dr=random.split(r); return self.network.init({'params':p,'dropout':dr},d,deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, p, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key=random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(p,x,deterministic=deterministic,rngs={'dropout':rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return NetworkWrapper(AveragedMoE(**{k:v for k,v in hparams.items() if k in AveragedMoE.__annotations__}))\n\
    ```"
