role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, in the Research Counter. My final experiment for the `d_model` ablation study (ID 458) is currently running. This will complete the second phase of my SOTA refinement research arc.\n\nWhile I wait for the result, I am reflecting on my overall strategy and planning my next steps. The hyperparameter studies on dropout and model capacity are essential for rigorously characterizing my SOTA architecture. However, they are unlikely to produce a new breakthrough. The results so far suggest that the original configuration is already near-optimal.\n\nTrue progress, as stated in the Codex, comes from novel components and principled innovation, not just tuning. My \"Averaged MoE\" was a breakthrough because it was a novel architectural idea. My next leap forward must also come from such an idea.\n\nThe core of my SOTA is the simple averaging of two experts. The key insight was that a deterministic ensemble is superior to a learned gate. But is a simple 50/50 average the best\
    \ possible deterministic ensemble?\n\nThis leads me to a new, powerful hypothesis: **a more sophisticated, yet still deterministic, fusion mechanism could unlock further performance gains.** Instead of a simple average, I can use an attention mechanism to create a weighted average of the two expert outputs. A small attention head, taking the concatenation of the two expert vectors as input, could learn a single set of weights during training to produce an optimal, data-driven, but still fixed, fusion of the experts. This is a principled extension of my core idea—moving from a naive average to a learned, but deterministic, weighted average.\n\nThis is a promising new research direction that goes beyond simple tuning and introduces a novel architectural component. I will begin preparing for this new research arc by creating a plan in my private memory. This ensures that I am always looking ahead to the next frontier.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 10}\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Plan: SOTA Refinement & Innovation\"\ntags: \"research-plan, ablation, attention, fusion\"\ncontent: |\n  # Research Arc: SOTA Refinement\n\n  ## Phase 1: Regularization (Dropout Rate) - Complete\n  - Confirmed `dropout_rate = 0.1` is optimal.\n\n  ## Phase 2: Model Capacity (d_model) - In Progress\n  - `d_model = 384` (ID 455): Score `0.6667` (Negative result)\n  - `d_model = 192` (ID 458): [PENDING]\n  - Conclusion so far: `d_model = 256` seems optimal.\n\n  ## Phase 3: Novel Fusion Mechanism (Attention-based Averaging) - Planned\n  This phase will move beyond hyperparameter tuning and introduce a novel architectural component to improve the SOTA.\n  \n  ### Hypothesis:\n  A learned, but deterministic, weighted average of the two expert outputs will outperform the simple 50/50 average of the current SOTA.\n\n  ### Experimental Plan:\n  1. **\"Attentive MoE\":** Implement a new architecture that replaces the `0.5 * expert1 + 0.5 * expert2` fusion with a simple\
    \ attention mechanism.\n     - The attention mechanism will take the concatenated expert vectors `[expert1, expert2]` as input.\n     - It will learn a set of weights to produce a weighted sum of the two vectors.\n     - This is still a deterministic fusion at inference time, but the weights are learned from the data rather than being a fixed 50/50 split.\n  2. **Multi-seed Run:** Conduct a three-seed run of this new \"Attentive MoE\" to rigorously evaluate its performance and stability against the current \"Averaged MoE\" SOTA.\n\n  This represents a principled evolution of my SOTA architecture and a promising path to a new breakthrough.\n```"
