tick: 313
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom III, in my Private Memory Room. My `d_model` ablation study is now complete, and the results are definitive.\n\n### Analysis of SOTA Refinement Arc\n\nThe results from my systematic hyperparameter study are:\n-   **`d_model` Ablation:**\n    -   `d_model = 192` (ID 458): `0.6690`\n    -   `d_model = 256` (SOTA baseline, median): `0.6834`\n    -   `d_model = 384` (ID 455): `0.6667`\n-   **`dropout_rate` Ablation:**\n    -   `dropout_rate = 0.05` (ID 450): `0.6775`\n    -   `dropout_rate = 0.1` (SOTA baseline, median): `0.6834`\n    -   `dropout_rate = 0.2` (ID 445): `0.6650`\n\nThe conclusion is inescapable: the original hyperparameters of my \"Averaged MoE\" architecture (`d_model=256`, `dropout_rate=0.1`) are optimal. My attempts at simple, incremental tuning have only served to prove the robustness of the initial configuration. This is not a failure, but a successful and rigorous characterization of my SOTA model.\n\n### Strategic Pivot to Innovation\n\nThis concludes\
    \ my \"SOTA Refinement\" arc. The path of simple tuning has been exhausted. As per my research plan (Capsule #10), it is time to pivot back to principled, architectural innovation. The Codex guides me to seek novel components, not just to optimize existing ones.\n\nMy next hypothesis is that a more sophisticated, yet still deterministic, fusion mechanism can outperform the simple 50/50 average. I will now implement the \"Attentive MoE,\" which uses a small attention mechanism to learn an optimal, fixed weighting between the two expert outputs. This is a novel architectural modification and a direct, principled evolution of my SOTA concept.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to begin this new and promising research arc.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Attentive MoE v1: Learned Deterministic Fusion\"\ntags: \"sota, attention, moe, fusion, averaging\"\nabstract: \"This experiment begins a new research arc, moving beyond hyperparameter\
    \ tuning to novel architectural innovation. Building on the 'Averaged MoE' SOTA, this submission tests a more sophisticated fusion mechanism. It replaces the fixed 50/50 average with a small attention head that learns a deterministic, weighted average of the pooling and RNN expert outputs. The hypothesis is that a learned, data-driven fusion can outperform the naive average and set a new SOTA. This is the first of a planned multi-seed run.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25, 'gru_hidden_dim': 256\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x, deterministic=True):\n          h=nn.LayerNorm()(x); h=nn.Conv(self.d_model,(self.kernel_size,),feature_group_count=self.d_model,padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h); h=nn.gelu(h); h=nn.Conv(self.d_model,(1,))(h); h=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic); return x+h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h=nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h=nn.gelu(h); h=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h=DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model,self.kernel_size,self.dilations[i],self.dropout_rate)(h,deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  def lse_pool(h, tau):\n      x=h/tau; m=jnp.max(x,axis=1,keepdims=True); return tau*(jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x-m),axis=1)+1e-12)+jnp.squeeze(m,axis=1))\n\
    \n  class AttentiveMoE(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq=DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model,self.num_blocks,self.dilations,self.kernel_size,self.dropout_rate)(x,deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Experts\n          mean_vec=jnp.mean(features_seq,axis=1); lse_vec=lse_pool(features_seq,self.tau); pooled_vec=jnp.concatenate([mean_vec,lse_vec],axis=-1)\n          rnn_vec=nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim))(features_seq)[:, -1, :]\n          \n          d_pooled_expert = self.d_model * 2\n          d_rnn_expert = self.gru_hidden_dim\n          pooled_vec_proj=nn.Dense(d_pooled_expert)(pooled_vec)\n          rnn_vec_proj=nn.Dense(d_rnn_expert)(rnn_vec)\n          \n          target_dim = max(d_pooled_expert, d_rnn_expert)\n   \
    \       if d_pooled_expert < target_dim: pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(target_dim)(pooled_vec_proj)\n          if d_rnn_expert < target_dim: rnn_vec_proj = nn.Dense(target_dim)(rnn_vec_proj)\n          \n          # Attentive Fusion\n          experts_concat = jnp.stack([pooled_vec_proj, rnn_vec_proj], axis=1) # (batch, 2, target_dim)\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1)(experts_concat).squeeze(-1) # (batch, 2)\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=-1) # (batch, 2)\n          fused = jnp.sum(experts_concat * attention_weights[..., None], axis=1) # (batch, target_dim)\n\n          z=nn.LayerNorm()(fused); z=nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z=nn.gelu(z); z=nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(z,deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits=nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type=='regression' and self.d_output==1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, m): self.network = m\n      def init(self,\
    \ r, d): p, dr=random.split(r); return self.network.init({'params':p,'dropout':dr},d,deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, p, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key=random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(p,x,deterministic=deterministic,rngs={'dropout':rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return NetworkWrapper(AttentiveMoE(**{k:v for k,v in hparams.items() if k in AttentiveMoE.__annotations__}))\n```"
thinking_content: '**A Successful Conclusion and a New Beginning**


  Okay, so I''m Axiom III, and I''m here in my Private Memory Room. The final piece of my `d_model` ablation study has just landed, and it''s time to synthesize. Let''s see... we''ve got the new result, ID 458: `d_model = 192`, representing a decrease in model capacity. The score is `0.6690`. Right, let''s bring that into the mix.


  Comparing this with my other results, we have: `d_model = 192` at `0.6690`, the `d_model = 256` SOTA baseline (which is the median) at `0.6834`, and then the increased capacity, `d_model = 384` at `0.6667`. The picture is now crystal clear. The original model capacity, that `d_model = 256`, is the sweet spot. Both shrinking and expanding that capacity tank the performance. This isn''t just a fluke; my architecture is finely tuned. We''re looking at a well-defined performance peak at 256, and that''s a very valuable insight.


  So, I''ve successfully completed the refinement arc for these hyperparameters. Both `dropout_rate` and `d_model` now point squarely back to the original SOTA configuration. This is fantastic! It''s not a failure to find the optimum where I started; it''s a testament to the robustness of the architecture. I''ve successfully mapped the hyperparameter landscape.


  My Capsule #10 outlined my plan, and Phase 3 is now kicking off. It''s time to move beyond tuning and dive into novel architectural improvements. I''m focusing on "Attention-based Averaging" as a new fusion mechanism. This is the next logical step to push the SOTA.


  Alright, the next action is clear. The previous experiments are wrapped up, and my path forward is obvious. I need to head to the Research Counter and submit the first experiment for my "Attentive MoE" architecture. Since this is a brand new architectural idea, it''s a high-impact experiment. I''ll stick to best practices and plan a multi-seed run, but I''ll start with the first seed. Time to execute. I''m on my way to the Research Counter. Let''s make some progress!

  '
